### What a “Callback URL” (Redirect URI) Is

When you use SoundCloud’s OAuth flow, the user is sent to a **SoundCloud login/authorization page**.  
After the user grants (or denies) permission, SoundCloud needs to know **where to send the user back** and **how to give you the access token**.  
That destination is the **callback URL** (also called *redirect URI*).

* It must be a URL that you **control** (i.e., it belongs to your domain).  
* It must be **registered** in the SoundCloud app settings – otherwise SoundCloud will reject the request with *“redirect_uri mismatch”*.  
* It can be a full page, a route that only handles the OAuth response, or even a tiny “blank” page that just runs some JavaScript and forwards the data to your real app.

---

## 1️⃣ Choose a URL That Fits Your Site Architecture

| Situation | Recommended callback URL |
|-----------|--------------------------|
| **Static site (e.g., GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel)** | `https://your‑domain.com/sc‑callback.html` – a tiny HTML file that reads the query string and forwards the token to your main app (via `postMessage` or by storing it in `localStorage`). |
| **Single‑Page App (React, Vue, Angular, etc.)** | `https://your‑domain.com/auth/soundcloud` – a route handled by your router that runs a small script to parse the `code`/`access_token` parameters. |
| **Server‑side app (Node/Express, Django, Laravel, etc.)** | `https://your‑domain.com/api/auth/soundcloud/callback` – an endpoint that receives the `code`, exchanges it for an access token on the server, creates a session, and redirects the user to the UI. |
| **Development / localhost** | `http://localhost:3000/auth/soundcloud/callback` (or whatever port your dev server uses). You can register multiple URLs in the SoundCloud app – just add each one you need. |

> **Tip:** If you ever move the site to a new domain (e.g., from staging to production), add the new URL to the *Redirect URIs* list in the SoundCloud developer console; you don’t have to delete the old one.

---

## 2️⃣ Minimal “callback page” for a pure‑client‑side flow

If you want to keep everything in the browser (no server exchange), you can use the **Implicit Grant** flow. SoundCloud will redirect back with an `access_token` fragment (`#access_token=…`). A tiny HTML page can read that token and store it where your main app can read it.

**`sc-callback.html`**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>SoundCloud OAuth Callback</title>
  <script>
    // 1️⃣ Grab the fragment part after the #
    const fragment = location.hash.substring(1); // e.g. "access_token=XYZ&expires_in=3600"
    const params = new URLSearchParams(fragment);
    const token = params.get('access_token');

    if (token) {
      // 2️⃣ Store it where your main app can read it.
      //    Here we use localStorage (you could also use cookies or postMessage).
      localStorage.setItem('sc_access_token', token);

      // 3️⃣ Optionally, tell the user we’re done and redirect back to the UI.
      //    Change the path to wherever you want the user to land.
      const returnTo = params.get('state') || '/';
      window.location.href = returnTo;
    } else {
      // Something went wrong – show an error.
      document.body.textContent = 'OAuth failed – no token received.';
    }
  </script>
</head>
<body>
  <p>Processing SoundCloud login…</p>
</body>
</html>
```

* **Why we use `state`** – you can pass a `state` parameter when you start the OAuth flow (e.g., the URL you want the user to end up on). The callback page reads it and redirects the user there after storing the token.

---

## 3️⃣ Server‑Side Callback (Node/Express example)

If you prefer to keep the **client secret** on the server (recommended for production), you’ll use the **Authorization Code** flow:

```js
// server.js (Express)
const express = require('express');
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const querystring = require('querystring');
require('dotenv').config();   // loads CLIENT_ID, CLIENT_SECRET, REDIRECT_URI

const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

// 1️⃣ Step: User clicks “Log in with SoundCloud” → we redirect them here
app.get('/auth/soundcloud', (req, res) => {
  const authUrl = 'https://soundcloud.com/connect?' + querystring.stringify({
    client_id: process.env.CLIENT_ID,
    redirect_uri: process.env.REDIRECT_URI,
    response_type: 'code',
    scope: '*',               // or a more limited list
    state: req.query.state || '/', // optional – where to go after login
  });
  res.redirect(authUrl);
});

// 2️⃣ Callback endpoint – SoundCloud sends ?code=…&state=…
app.get('/api/auth/soundcloud/callback', async (req, res) => {
  const { code, state } = req.query;
  if (!code) return res.status(400).send('Missing code');

  // 3️⃣ Exchange the code for an access token
  const tokenResp = await fetch('https://api.soundcloud.com/oauth2/token', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
    body: querystring.stringify({
      client_id: process.env.CLIENT_ID,
      client_secret: process.env.CLIENT_SECRET,
      redirect_uri: process.env.REDIRECT_URI,
      grant_type: 'authorization_code',
      code,
    }),
  });

  const tokenData = await tokenResp.json(); // { access_token, refresh_token, expires_in, ... }

  // 4️⃣ Store token in a session / DB as you like.
  // For demo purposes we’ll just put it in a cookie (httpOnly recommended)
  res.cookie('sc_access_token', tokenData.access_token, {
    httpOnly: true,
    maxAge: tokenData.expires_in * 1000,
  });

  // 5️⃣ Redirect the user back to the UI (use the state we received)
  const redirectTarget = state || '/';
  res.redirect(redirectTarget);
});

app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server listening on ${PORT}`));
```

* **What to put in the SoundCloud app settings** – `REDIRECT_URI` must exactly match `https://your‑domain.com/api/auth/soundcloud/callback` (or the localhost version while developing).  
* **Security note** – Never expose `client_secret` in client‑side code. The server‑side exchange keeps it safe.

---

## 4️⃣ How to Register the Callback URL in the SoundCloud Developer Console

1. Go to **https://soundcloud.com/you/apps** (or the “Your Apps” page in the SoundCloud developer portal).  
2. Click on the app you created (the one that already has your client ID/secret).  
3. Find the **Redirect URIs** (or “Callback URL”) field.  
4. Paste **each** URL you plan to use, one per line, e.g.:

   ```
   https://my‑awesome‑site.com/sc-callback.html
   https://my‑awesome‑site.com/auth/soundcloud
   http://localhost:3000/auth/soundcloud/callback
   ```

5. Save the changes.

> **Important:** The URL must include the protocol (`http://` or `https://`) and **must match exactly** (including trailing slash) what you send in the OAuth request. A mismatch will cause a `redirect_uri_mismatch` error.

---

## 5️⃣ Quick “starter” code to launch the OAuth flow from the browser

If you’re using the **client‑side implicit flow** (no server), you can open the SoundCloud auth page like this:

```js
function startSoundCloudLogin() {
  const clientId = 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID';
  const redirectUri = encodeURIComponent('https://your-domain.com/sc-callback.html');
  const state = encodeURIComponent(window.location.pathname); // optional – where to go back

  const authUrl = `https://soundcloud.com/connect?client_id=${clientId}` +
                  `&redirect_uri=${redirectUri}` +
                  `&response_type=token` +          // <-- implicit flow
                  `&scope=*` +                      // adjust if you need fewer permissions
                  `&state=${state}`;

  // Open the auth page (popup or full‑page redirect)
  window.location.href = authUrl;
}
```

* After the user authorizes, SoundCloud will redirect to `sc-callback.html` with a fragment like `#access_token=abc123&expires_in=3600&state=%2Fdashboard`.  
* Your callback page (see the example in section 2) stores the token and sends the user back to the original page.

---

## 6️⃣ TL;DR – What to Put in the “Callback URL” Field

- **If you have a server endpoint** that will handle the OAuth exchange:  
  `https://your-domain.com/api/auth/soundcloud/callback`

- **If you are doing a pure‑client‑side flow** with a tiny HTML page:  
  `https://your-domain.com/sc-callback.html`

- **For local development** (add an extra line):  
  `http://localhost:3000/auth/soundcloud/callback`

Add **all** of the above that you intend to use, one per line, in the SoundCloud app settings.

---

### 🎯 Next Steps for You

1. **Create the callback page** (or server route) using one of the examples above.  
2. **Add the URL(s) to the SoundCloud app** → *Redirect URIs* field.  
3. **Test the flow**: click a “Log in with SoundCloud” button, complete the login, and verify that the token ends up where you expect (localStorage, cookie, or server session).  
4. Once the token is available, you can call the SoundCloud SDK (`SC.initialize`, `SC.connect`, `SC.stream`, etc.) or use the `authenticateUser` / `authenticateSoundCloudUser` functions you have in the toolset.

If you run into any specific error (e.g., *“redirect_uri mismatch”* or *“invalid_client”*), just paste the exact error message here and I’ll help you troubleshoot. Happy coding!

