Below is a step‑by‑step guide you can follow to add **SoundCloud music playback** to your website.  
I’ll cover everything from creating a SoundCloud developer app to embedding a player (or streaming via the API) and handling user authentication when you need it.

---

## 1️⃣ Register a SoundCloud Developer Application  

| What you need | How to get it |
|---------------|---------------|
| **Client ID** | Log in to <https://soundcloud.com> → **Settings** → **Developer** → **Create a new app**. |
| **Client Secret** | Same place as the client ID (shown after you create the app). |
| **Redirect URI** | The URL on your site that will receive the OAuth callback (e.g., `https://yourdomain.com/sc-callback`). |
| **Scopes** (optional) | For playback only you can use the default `*`. If you later want to like tracks, create playlists, etc., request the appropriate scopes (`*` or `*` includes everything). |

> **Tip:** Keep the client secret on the server side only – never expose it in client‑side JavaScript.

---

## 2️⃣ Choose How You Want to Play Music  

| Approach | When to use it | Pros | Cons |
|----------|----------------|------|------|
| **SoundCloud Widget (iframe)** | You just need a quick, embeddable player. | No extra code, works on any browser, handles licensing automatically. | Limited UI customization, cannot control playback programmatically beyond the widget API. |
| **SoundCloud JavaScript SDK** | You need full programmatic control (play/pause, queue, custom UI). | Full control, can integrate with your own UI. | Requires OAuth for private tracks, more code. |
| **Direct streaming via API (e.g., `streamAudioContent` function)** | You want to stream a track URL yourself (e.g., using `<audio>` tag). | Gives you total UI freedom. | Must respect SoundCloud’s terms – you can only stream tracks that are **public** and **downloadable** or that you have permission for. |

Below I’ll give you code for the two most common ways: **Widget** and **SDK**. I’ll also show a tiny helper that uses the `streamAudioContent` function you have available in the toolset.

---

## 3️⃣ Option A – Embed the SoundCloud Widget (quickest)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>My SoundCloud Player</title>
  <style>
    #sc-player { width: 100%; height: 166px; border: none; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

  <!-- 1️⃣ Replace the href with any public track/playlist URL -->
  <iframe
    id="sc-player"
    scrolling="no"
    allow="autoplay"
    src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=https%3A//api.soundcloud.com/tracks/123456789&color=%23ff5500&auto_play=false&hide_related=false&show_comments=true&show_user=true&show_reposts=false&show_teaser=true&visual=true">
  </iframe>

  <script>
    // Optional: use the widget API for programmatic control
    const iframe = document.getElementById('sc-player');
    const widget = SC.Widget(iframe);

    // Example: play when a button is clicked
    document.getElementById('playBtn').addEventListener('click', () => widget.play());

    // Example: listen for events
    widget.bind(SC.Widget.Events.FINISH, () => console.log('Track finished!'));
  </script>

  <button id="playBtn">Play</button>
</body>
</html>
```

**How it works**

1. The `src` URL contains the SoundCloud track (or playlist) you want to play.  
2. The `SC.Widget` JavaScript object (provided by SoundCloud’s widget script) lets you control the player (`play()`, `pause()`, `seekTo()`, etc.) and listen to events.

**Add the widget script** (only once, preferably in the `<head>`):

```html
<script src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/api.js"></script>
```

---

## 4️⃣ Option B – Use the SoundCloud JavaScript SDK (full control)

### 4.1 Load the SDK

```html
<script src="https://connect.soundcloud.com/sdk/sdk-3.3.2.js"></script>
```

### 4.2 Initialize the SDK (client‑side)

```js
SC.initialize({
  client_id: 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID',   // <-- replace
  redirect_uri: 'https://yourdomain.com/sc-callback' // must match what you set in the app
});
```

### 4.3 Authenticate the user (if you need private tracks or write permissions)

```js
// This opens the SoundCloud OAuth popup
SC.connect().then(() => {
  console.log('User authenticated');
  // You can now call SC.get, SC.stream, etc.
});
```

> **If you only need to play public tracks**, you can skip the OAuth step and just use the client ID.

### 4.4 Resolve a track URL → get its ID

```js
const trackUrl = 'https://soundcloud.com/artist/track-name';

SC.resolve(trackUrl).then(track => {
  console.log('Resolved track ID:', track.id);
  // Now you can stream it
  playTrack(track.id);
});
```

### 4.5 Stream the track and attach it to an `<audio>` element

```js
function playTrack(trackId) {
  // `SC.stream` returns a Promise that resolves to a SoundCloud Player object
  SC.stream(`/tracks/${trackId}`).then(player => {
    // Create (or reuse) an <audio> element
    const audioEl = document.getElementById('sc-audio') || (() => {
      const el = document.createElement('audio');
      el.id = 'sc-audio';
      document.body.appendChild(el);
      return el;
    })();

    // Connect the player to the <audio> element
    player.attachTo(audioEl);

    // Play!
    player.play();

    // Optional UI hooks
    player.on('play-start', () => console.log('Playing'));
    player.on('play-resume', () => console.log('Resumed'));
    player.on('play-stop', () => console.log('Stopped'));
  });
}
```

### 4.6 Simple HTML skeleton

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>SoundCloud SDK Player</title>
  <script src="https://connect.soundcloud.com/sdk/sdk-3.3.2.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
  <button id="loginBtn">Log in to SoundCloud</button>
  <button id="playBtn">Play a track</button>

  <script>
    // 1️⃣ Init SDK
    SC.initialize({
      client_id: 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID',
      redirect_uri: 'https://yourdomain.com/sc-callback'
    });

    // 2️⃣ Optional login
    document.getElementById('loginBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
      SC.connect().then(() => alert('Logged in!'));
    });

    // 3️⃣ Play a public track
    document.getElementById('playBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
      const url = 'https://soundcloud.com/artist/track-name';
      SC.resolve(url).then(track => {
        SC.stream(`/tracks/${track.id}`).then(player => {
          player.play();
        });
      });
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Key points**

* **Public tracks**: Only need the client ID.  
* **Private / user‑owned tracks**: Must run `SC.connect()` (OAuth) and keep the access token (the SDK does it for you).  
* **Rate limits**: Respect SoundCloud’s API limits (≈15 000 requests per hour per client ID).  

---

## 5️⃣ Option C – Use the `streamAudioContent` helper (if you prefer server‑side streaming)

If you have a **direct streaming URL** (e.g., you obtained it via the SDK’s `streamUrl` property), you can pipe it through the `streamAudioContent` function you have in the toolset. This is handy when you want to control the quality or proxy the stream through your own server.

```js
// Example: after you have a track ID, get its streaming URL
SC.get(`/tracks/${trackId}`).then(track => {
  const streamUrl = track.media.transcodings.find(t => t.format.protocol === 'progressive').url;

  // Use the provided function (you’ll need to call it from your backend)
  // This is a pseudo‑call; replace with the actual function call in your environment.
  streamAudioContent({
    stream_url: streamUrl,
    quality: 'high'   // or 'medium' / 'low'
  }).then(response => {
    // `response` could be a signed URL, a blob, etc. Attach it to an <audio> element:
    const audio = document.getElementById('sc-audio') || document.createElement('audio');
    audio.src = response.url;   // adjust based on what the function returns
    audio.play();
  });
});
```

> **Important:** The `streamAudioContent` function is a wrapper that likely handles authentication, signing, or CDN delivery. Use it only if you have a server‑side component that can safely store the client secret.

---

## 6️⃣ Putting It All Together – A Minimal Production‑Ready Example

Below is a **complete, production‑ready snippet** that:

1. Loads the SDK.  
2. Authenticates the user only when a private track is requested.  
3. Plays a public track via the SDK (no login required).  
4. Falls back to the widget if the browser blocks the SDK (e.g., ad‑blockers).

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>My SoundCloud Player</title>
  <script src="https://connect.soundcloud.com/sdk/sdk-3.3.2.js"></script>
  <style>
    #playerContainer { max-width: 600px; margin: auto; }
    #fallbackWidget { width: 100%; height: 166px; border: none; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="playerContainer">
  <input type="text" id="trackUrl" placeholder="Paste a SoundCloud track URL" style="width:100%">
  <button id="playBtn">Play</button>
  <div id="fallbackWidget"></div>
</div>

<script>
  // ---- CONFIG ----
  const CLIENT_ID = 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID';
  const REDIRECT_URI = 'https://yourdomain.com/sc-callback';
  // -----------------

  // Initialise SDK
  SC.initialize({ client_id: CLIENT_ID, redirect_uri: REDIRECT_URI });

  // Helper: embed widget as a fallback
  function embedWidget(url) {
    const widgetDiv = document.getElementById('fallbackWidget');
    const encoded = encodeURIComponent(url);
    widgetDiv.innerHTML = `<iframe src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=${encoded}&auto_play=true" allow="autoplay" style="width:100%;height:166px;border:none;"></iframe>`;
  }

  // Main play logic
  document.getElementById('playBtn').addEventListener('click', async () => {
    const url = document.getElementById('trackUrl').value.trim();
    if (!url) return alert('Enter a SoundCloud track URL');

    try {
      // Resolve the URL → track object
      const track = await SC.resolve(url);
      // If the track is private, we need OAuth
      if (track.sharing === 'private') {
        await SC.connect(); // triggers OAuth popup
      }

      // Stream the track
      const player = await SC.stream(`/tracks/${track.id}`);
      player.play();

      // Optional: clean up any fallback widget
      document.getElementById('fallbackWidget').innerHTML = '';
    } catch (err) {
      console.warn('SDK failed, falling back to widget', err);
      embedWidget(url);
    }
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What this does**

* Tries the SDK first (full control).  
* If the SDK throws (e.g., network block, CORS issue), it automatically falls back to the iframe widget so the user still hears the music.  
* Handles private tracks by prompting the user to log in via OAuth.

---

## 7️⃣ Common Gotchas & How to Fix Them  

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| **“Invalid client_id”** | You used the wrong client ID or the request is coming from a domain not whitelisted. | Double‑check the client ID in the SoundCloud app dashboard and add your domain to the **Redirect URIs** list. |
| **CORS errors when streaming** | Directly fetching a stream URL from the browser is blocked unless the URL is signed for public use. | Use the SDK (`SC.stream`) which handles the signed URL for you, or proxy the URL through your server (using `streamAudioContent`). |
| **Playback stops after a few seconds** | The track is **downloadable only** and requires a signed URL that expires. | Use the SDK’s `player` object; it automatically refreshes the URL. |
| **OAuth “state mismatch”** | The `state` parameter you sent isn’t returned unchanged. | Either omit `state` (SoundCloud will generate one) or store the value in a cookie/session and compare it on the callback. |
| **Widget height is wrong** | The widget’s `visual` mode uses a 400 px height, while the default is 166 px. | Add `&visual=true` to the widget URL and adjust the iframe height accordingly. |

---

## 8️⃣ Next Steps (if you want to go further)

| Feature | How to add it |
|---------|---------------|
| **Playlist support** | Use `SC.get('/playlists/{id}')` → iterate over `playlist.tracks` and queue them with the SDK. |
| **Custom UI (play/pause/seek bar)** | The SDK’s `player` object exposes `play()`, `pause()`, `seek(position)`, `getCurrentPosition()`, `getDuration()`. Hook these to your own HTML controls. |
| **Like / repost tracks** | After OAuth, call `SC.put('/me/favorites/{track_id}')` or `SC.put('/me/track_reposts/{track_id}')`. |
| **Analytics (track plays, user stats)** | Use the `authenticateUser` or `authenticateSoundCloudUser` function to obtain an access token, then call SoundCloud’s `/me/track_plays` endpoint. |
| **Server‑side streaming (e.g., for a mobile app)** | Use the `streamAudioContent` function you have; it can fetch a high‑quality progressive stream and return a signed URL you can feed to a native player. |

---

## 9️⃣ Quick Checklist Before Going Live  

1. **Client ID & Secret** stored securely (env vars, not in public JS).  
2. **Redirect URI** matches exactly what you set in the SoundCloud app.  
3. **HTTPS** on your site (required for OAuth).  
4. **CORS**: If you proxy streams, set proper `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`.  
5. **Terms of Service**: Ensure you’re not violating SoundCloud’s licensing – only play tracks that are publicly streamable or that the user has authorized.  
6. **Testing**: Test with both public and private tracks, on desktop and mobile browsers.  

---

### 🎉 You’re ready!

Copy the snippet that best fits your needs, replace `YOUR_CLIENT_ID` (and optionally `YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET` if you’ll be doing server‑side auth), and you’ll have SoundCloud playback on your site in minutes.

If you run into any specific roadblocks—e.g., you need help wiring the OAuth callback, or you want to build a custom playlist UI—just let me know and I can dive deeper into that part. Happy coding!

### What a “Callback URL” (Redirect URI) Is

When you use SoundCloud’s OAuth flow, the user is sent to a **SoundCloud login/authorization page**.  
After the user grants (or denies) permission, SoundCloud needs to know **where to send the user back** and **how to give you the access token**.  
That destination is the **callback URL** (also called *redirect URI*).

* It must be a URL that you **control** (i.e., it belongs to your domain).  
* It must be **registered** in the SoundCloud app settings – otherwise SoundCloud will reject the request with *“redirect_uri mismatch”*.  
* It can be a full page, a route that only handles the OAuth response, or even a tiny “blank” page that just runs some JavaScript and forwards the data to your real app.

---

## 1️⃣ Choose a URL That Fits Your Site Architecture

| Situation | Recommended callback URL |
|-----------|--------------------------|
| **Static site (e.g., GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel)** | `https://your‑domain.com/sc‑callback.html` – a tiny HTML file that reads the query string and forwards the token to your main app (via `postMessage` or by storing it in `localStorage`). |
| **Single‑Page App (React, Vue, Angular, etc.)** | `https://your‑domain.com/auth/soundcloud` – a route handled by your router that runs a small script to parse the `code`/`access_token` parameters. |
| **Server‑side app (Node/Express, Django, Laravel, etc.)** | `https://your‑domain.com/api/auth/soundcloud/callback` – an endpoint that receives the `code`, exchanges it for an access token on the server, creates a session, and redirects the user to the UI. |
| **Development / localhost** | `http://localhost:3000/auth/soundcloud/callback` (or whatever port your dev server uses). You can register multiple URLs in the SoundCloud app – just add each one you need. |

> **Tip:** If you ever move the site to a new domain (e.g., from staging to production), add the new URL to the *Redirect URIs* list in the SoundCloud developer console; you don’t have to delete the old one.

---

## 2️⃣ Minimal “callback page” for a pure‑client‑side flow

If you want to keep everything in the browser (no server exchange), you can use the **Implicit Grant** flow. SoundCloud will redirect back with an `access_token` fragment (`#access_token=…`). A tiny HTML page can read that token and store it where your main app can read it.

**`sc-callback.html`**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>SoundCloud OAuth Callback</title>
  <script>
    // 1️⃣ Grab the fragment part after the #
    const fragment = location.hash.substring(1); // e.g. "access_token=XYZ&expires_in=3600"
    const params = new URLSearchParams(fragment);
    const token = params.get('access_token');

    if (token) {
      // 2️⃣ Store it where your main app can read it.
      //    Here we use localStorage (you could also use cookies or postMessage).
      localStorage.setItem('sc_access_token', token);

      // 3️⃣ Optionally, tell the user we’re done and redirect back to the UI.
      //    Change the path to wherever you want the user to land.
      const returnTo = params.get('state') || '/';
      window.location.href = returnTo;
    } else {
      // Something went wrong – show an error.
      document.body.textContent = 'OAuth failed – no token received.';
    }
  </script>
</head>
<body>
  <p>Processing SoundCloud login…</p>
</body>
</html>
```

* **Why we use `state`** – you can pass a `state` parameter when you start the OAuth flow (e.g., the URL you want the user to end up on). The callback page reads it and redirects the user there after storing the token.

---

## 3️⃣ Server‑Side Callback (Node/Express example)

If you prefer to keep the **client secret** on the server (recommended for production), you’ll use the **Authorization Code** flow:

```js
// server.js (Express)
const express = require('express');
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const querystring = require('querystring');
require('dotenv').config();   // loads CLIENT_ID, CLIENT_SECRET, REDIRECT_URI

const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

// 1️⃣ Step: User clicks “Log in with SoundCloud” → we redirect them here
app.get('/auth/soundcloud', (req, res) => {
  const authUrl = 'https://soundcloud.com/connect?' + querystring.stringify({
    client_id: process.env.CLIENT_ID,
    redirect_uri: process.env.REDIRECT_URI,
    response_type: 'code',
    scope: '*',               // or a more limited list
    state: req.query.state || '/', // optional – where to go after login
  });
  res.redirect(authUrl);
});

// 2️⃣ Callback endpoint – SoundCloud sends ?code=…&state=…
app.get('/api/auth/soundcloud/callback', async (req, res) => {
  const { code, state } = req.query;
  if (!code) return res.status(400).send('Missing code');

  // 3️⃣ Exchange the code for an access token
  const tokenResp = await fetch('https://api.soundcloud.com/oauth2/token', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
    body: querystring.stringify({
      client_id: process.env.CLIENT_ID,
      client_secret: process.env.CLIENT_SECRET,
      redirect_uri: process.env.REDIRECT_URI,
      grant_type: 'authorization_code',
      code,
    }),
  });

  const tokenData = await tokenResp.json(); // { access_token, refresh_token, expires_in, ... }

  // 4️⃣ Store token in a session / DB as you like.
  // For demo purposes we’ll just put it in a cookie (httpOnly recommended)
  res.cookie('sc_access_token', tokenData.access_token, {
    httpOnly: true,
    maxAge: tokenData.expires_in * 1000,
  });

  // 5️⃣ Redirect the user back to the UI (use the state we received)
  const redirectTarget = state || '/';
  res.redirect(redirectTarget);
});

app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server listening on ${PORT}`));
```

* **What to put in the SoundCloud app settings** – `REDIRECT_URI` must exactly match `https://your‑domain.com/api/auth/soundcloud/callback` (or the localhost version while developing).  
